      "title_s":["One Model, Two Processes: Unifying Aerospace ARP4754B Development and ARP4761A Safety Assessment in a SysML v2 Source of Truth"],
      "abstract_s":["ARP4754B aerospace system development and ARP4761A safety assessment are written as one interconnected iterative process, but on real programmes they are often run by separate teams on separate tools: architecture development lives in conventional system/MBSE documentation, while the safety assessments in spreadsheets and Fault Trees. Each iteration crosses a manual export/import step where traceability quietly decays. This paper argues that both processes should operate on a single model, and that the textual notation of SysML v2 should facilitate this: one textual notation, version-controlled model in which definitions can be created such as functions, logical architecture, hazards, failure conditions and requirements which are peer elements. The functional decomposition acts as the bridge between the disciplines, a shared metamodel with a separate validation engine keeps them consistent, and the applicable certification standard enters the model as a replaceable catalogue data. A workbench built this way is being developed and tested on open-source examples such as conventional fixed-wing aircraft and unmanned aircraft systems; the paper reports what it contains, what worked, and the qualification and acceptance questions it raises."],
      "halId_s":"hal-05752004",
